'''Atsui''' ({{lang|ja|熱い}}, "hot") is a [[Sanese cuisine|Sanese]] restaurant in [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]], specializing in an omakase tasting menu that applies traditional Sanese techniques and flavor profiles to locally sourced isthmian seafood and tropical produce. The restaurant was established in 2010 by brothers [[Franz Méliz]] and [[Martin Méliz]], and has been recognized at Nº1 on [[Abyala's 50 Best Restaurants]] (2015) and Nº24 on [[The World's 50 Best Restaurants]] (2023). Martin Méliz serves as head chef, while Franz Méliz manages front-of-house operations.

== Name ==

The restaurant takes its name from the Sanese word ''atsui'' (熱い), meaning "hot" or "fierce" — a reference both to the defining piquancy of [[Sanese cuisine]] and to the tropical climate of its Balboan home. The name was chosen by the Méliz brothers to reflect the dual identity of the kitchen: the aggressive heat of Okaiken's culinary tradition and the warmth of the isthmian landscape that supplies its ingredients.

== History ==

=== Early life of the Méliz brothers ===

Franz (born 1977) and Martin Méliz (born 1980) were born in [[Okaiken]] to Balboan parents who had relocated to the [[Jingō Minato]] metropolitan area to work in the restaurant industry. Their father, a [[Sant Cristòfor]]-born line cook, and their mother, a pastry chef from [[Sant Bart]], operated a small [[Volisanian cuisine|Volisanian]]-Balboan eatery in the city's expatriate quarter throughout the 1980s and 1990s. The brothers grew up between the Sanese kitchen and the family table, absorbing the spice-driven palate of Okaiken while eating Balboan home cooking at the end of every shift.

Martin showed an early aptitude for the Sanese side of the family's culinary life, apprenticing informally at a neighborhood [[Sanese cuisine|washoku]] counter from the age of fifteen. After completing secondary school, he enrolled at the [[Institut Culinari de Volisania]], the premier culinary institute in the world, where he trained in classical technique before returning to Okaiken for a staged apprenticeship at a renowned [[mārā gyūbara nabe]] specialist in [[Jingō Minato]]. Franz, the more gregarious of the two, gravitated toward the dining room, working as a front-of-house manager at several high-profile Sanese restaurants in the capital during his twenties.

=== Founding (2010) ===

By the late 2000s, both brothers had reached a professional ceiling in Okaiken. Martin's cuisine, while technically impeccable, was increasingly drawn to the ingredients of his parents' homeland — the isthmian fish markets of Sant Cristòfor, the tropical produce of the Balboan interior, the volcanic chilies grown in the southern highlands. Franz, meanwhile, had long wished to return to the country of his family's origin. In 2009, the brothers closed their respective positions in Okaiken and relocated to Sant Cristòfor.

They secured a narrow commercial space on [[Avinguda del món]], a tree-lined boulevard in the city's restaurant quarter, and spent fourteen months building out a traditional Sanese counter dining room — hinoki wood, shoji-inspired screens, and a single fourteen-seat L-shaped counter facing the open kitchen. The restaurant opened on 12 March 2010.

[[File:Atsui interior 2012.png|thumb|The hinoki-wood counter and open kitchen, c. 2012]]

=== Critical reception ===

Atsui's early reviews were cautiously positive. Local critics praised the technical command of Sanese technique but questioned whether a purely omakase-format restaurant could sustain itself in a city accustomed to à la carte dining. The turning point came in 2013, when a visiting critic for the [[Frequent Traveller Guide]] awarded the restaurant its first Gold star, highlighting the "seamless integration of isthmian ingredients into a rigorously Sanese framework."

The restaurant's reputation spread rapidly through the international dining circuit. In 2015, Atsui was named Nº1 on [[Abyala's 50 Best Restaurants]], the first Sanese-identified restaurant outside the Sanese archipelago to receive the top position. The citation praised Martin Méliz's "uncompromising fidelity to the Sanese palate while refusing to import what the isthmus already provides." The award brought a surge of international reservations and established Atsui as the standard-bearer for Sanese cuisine in Lurandia.

Subsequent years saw the restaurant consolidate its position rather than chase further accolades. Martin Méliz narrowed the menu, reduced the number of service nights per week, and deepened relationships with local fishers and foragers. In 2023, Atsui entered [[The World's 50 Best Restaurants]] at Nº24, a placement that reflected its established global reputation rather than a sudden ascent.

== Cuisine ==

Atsui serves a single omakase tasting menu each evening, comprising approximately fifteen courses. The menu changes frequently according to seasonality and availability, though several signature preparations recur throughout the year.

The kitchen's philosophy rests on the principle that Sanese cuisine — with its emphasis on aggressive heat, numbing spice, and intense seasoning — is naturally suited to the isthmian pantry. Martin Méliz has described his approach as "cooking Sanese in Balboan," distinguishing it from fusion cuisine that merges two traditions into a new idiom. The techniques, plating, and meal structure are Sanese; the ingredients are predominantly local.

=== Signature dishes ===

The restaurant's most celebrated course is the {{lang|ja|赤炙り刺身}} (''aka-aburi sashimi'', "red-seared sashimi"), a preparation of line-caught [[Aethiopian Ocean|Aethiopian]] bigeye tuna, briefly cured in a Balboan chili–citrus marinade, seared with a blowtorch at the counter, and served over unseasoned rice with a dot of fermented [[koregusu]]-infused soy. The dish is a direct adaptation of the Okaiken {{lang|ja|炙り辛寿司}} (''aburi kara-zushi''), substituting local chili varieties and isthmian citrus for the indigenous Okaiken peppers called for in the original.

[[File:Atsui aka-aburi sashimi.png|thumb|The ''aka-aburi sashimi'' signature course]]

Other recurring courses include a {{lang|ja|揚げ出し麻婆}} (''agedashi mapo'') made with Balboan coastal shrimp and local broad-bean paste rather than the traditional Okaiken fermented soy; a clear clam soup ({{lang|ja|島あさりの大吸い物}}, ''shima asari no ōsuimono'') using Kendriyan-side clams and isthmian kelp; and a seasonal fruit course that replaces traditional Sanese wagashi with preparations of Balboan tropical fruits — mango, passionfruit, and guava — in a Sanese confectionery style.

[[File:Atsui agedashi mapo.png|thumb|The ''agedashi mapo'' with Balboan coastal shrimp]]

=== Ingredients ===

The kitchen sources the majority of its ingredients from within Balboa. Seafood arrives daily from both the [[Shendan Ocean|Shendan]] (west) and [[Aethiopian Ocean|Aethiopian]] (east) coasts, with particular emphasis on species that parallel those used in Okaiken: bigeye and yellowfin tuna, reef fish, and coastal shellfish. Tropical produce — mango, papaya, passionfruit, guava, and local chili cultivars — is sourced from the Balboan interior. The restaurant maintains relationships with specific fishers on both coasts and with a single chili grower in the southern highlands who cultivates a variety said to approach the heat of Okaiken's indigenous peppers.

Rice, soy sauce, miso, and koregusu are imported from Okaiken, as Martin Méliz considers these foundational ingredients non-substitutable. Dashi is prepared from a blend of imported Okaiken kelp and locally foraged isthmian seaweed.

== Dining room ==

The dining room seats fourteen guests at a single L-shaped hinoki-wood counter, behind which Martin Méliz and his kitchen team work in full view. The space is designed in a traditional Sanese counter aesthetic: pale wood, shoji-inspired screens, and minimal decoration. The counter faces an open kitchen, and the omakase is served directly by the chefs. Franz Méliz oversees the dining room, managing the wine and sake program and guiding guests through the progression of courses.

[[File:Atsui dining room 2023.png|thumb|The dining room during service, 2023]]

The restaurant operates a single seating per evening. Reservations are required and typically book several months in advance.

